--- original description below, no longer accurate to current behavior ---

brcmfmac: also set apsta=1 on the primary interface (matching P2P's routing)

patches/862 forced the firmware "apsta" iovar to 1 (instead of the
stock 0) when a plain AP-role interface starts on a chip lacking
RSDB/MCHAN - but set it on the AP vif's OWN ifp. The only other in-tree
precedent for apsta=1 is p2p.c's brcmf_p2p_set_firmware(), which always
sets it via brcmf_get_ifp(cfg->pub, 0) - the PRIMARY interface
(bsscfgidx/ifidx 0) - never via whichever vif is actually using the
resulting concurrent mode. brcmf_fil_iovar_int_set()/_data_set() are
dispatched per-ifp (fwil.c), so this is a real routing difference, not
cosmetic: if firmware only honors this iovar when addressed via
bsscfgidx 0, setting it on a non-zero-ifidx AP vif (as patches/862 did)
would silently no-op.

This patch additionally sets apsta=1 via the primary interface
(brcmf_get_ifp(drvr, 0)), matching P2P's exact routing, alongside the
existing AP-vif-local set from patches/862 - belt and suspenders, since
which routing (if either) the firmware actually honors is unverified
without a live test.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/cfg80211.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/cfg80211.c
@@ -5199,6 +5199,12 @@
 				goto exit;
 			}
 			brcmf_fil_iovar_int_set(ifp, "apsta", 1);
+			if (ifp->ifidx != 0) {
+				struct brcmf_if *pri_ifp = brcmf_get_ifp(drvr, 0);
+
+				if (pri_ifp)
+					brcmf_fil_iovar_int_set(pri_ifp, "apsta", 1);
+			}
 		}
